-- The built-in connectives called through a function parameter.
--
-- `AND`, `OR`, `IMPLIES` and `NOT` are evaluated in frames of their own
-- (UNKNOWN-EVALUATION-SPEC §4.4, U2, U2b), and a variable that holds one --
-- here a parameter of `combine` or `apply` -- gets the same frames: the left
-- operand first, and the right one only if the left does not decide. The
-- short circuits below would otherwise divide by zero. No trace shows a
-- parameter named `a` or `b`, because the connectives have none.
--
-- The connectives are named here by their internal spelling, `__AND__` and
-- so on, which is the only way to pass one as a value.

GIVEN f IS A FUNCTION FROM BOOLEAN AND BOOLEAN TO BOOLEAN
      p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
combine f p q MEANS f p q

GIVEN g IS A FUNCTION FROM BOOLEAN TO BOOLEAN
      p IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
apply g p MEANS g p

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`has a positive reciprocal` n MEANS 1 DIVIDED BY n GREATER THAN 0

-- The truth tables, through the parameter.
#EVAL combine `__AND__` TRUE TRUE
#EVAL combine `__AND__` TRUE FALSE
#EVAL combine `__OR__` FALSE FALSE
#EVAL combine `__OR__` FALSE TRUE
#EVAL combine `__IMPLIES__` TRUE FALSE
#EVAL combine `__IMPLIES__` TRUE TRUE
#EVAL apply `__NOT__` TRUE
#EVAL apply `__NOT__` FALSE

-- The right operand is not evaluated when the left decides ...
#EVAL combine `__AND__` FALSE (`has a positive reciprocal` 0)
#EVAL combine `__OR__` TRUE (`has a positive reciprocal` 0)
#EVAL combine `__IMPLIES__` FALSE (`has a positive reciprocal` 0)
-- ... and is when it does not: this one divides by zero.
#EVAL combine `__AND__` TRUE (`has a positive reciprocal` 0)

-- The same calls written out, for comparison.
#EVAL FALSE AND `has a positive reciprocal` 0
#EVAL TRUE OR `has a positive reciprocal` 0

-- What a trace shows: the call, then the operands it evaluated.
#EVALTRACE combine `__AND__` TRUE FALSE
#EVALTRACE combine `__OR__` TRUE (`has a positive reciprocal` 0)
#EVALTRACE apply `__NOT__` TRUE
#EVALTRACE TRUE AND (FALSE OR `has a positive reciprocal` 2)
